// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries and conjectures of further number theory,
// MSC 2020 11C (polynomials and matrices over ℤ), 11K (probabilistic number theory: distribution modulo 1, discrepancy,
// normal numbers, metric theory of continued fractions, additive functions), 11L (exponential and character sums) and
// 11Y (computational number theory). In English. Built on 3 November 2026. Moved here by rule 1 of ../../CLAUDE.md,
// with its id: `mason-stothers` from ../../arithmetic-geometry/ (class 11C08).
//
// Model as in ../../algebraic-number-theory/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are
// the standard textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a
// proof that rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`). `area` is the three-character MSC class, as in
// data/concepts.js; "basic" holds two results of analytic number theory (11N) that arithmetic geometry does not have
// yet. `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// Results that hold under an unproved hypothesis (GRH, random-map heuristics) are theorems whose statement names the
// hypothesis; conjectures are never premises. Heuristic running times of sieve algorithms are stated in `text`.
// References to theorems of other folders: "ent:", "gt:", "ca:", "la:", "ant:", "ar:", "ag:", "ft:", "dio:", "ana:",
// "pr:", "ds:", "cs:".
//
// Sources: Kuipers and Niederreiter, Uniform Distribution of Sequences (1974); Drmota and Tichy, Sequences,
// Discrepancies and Applications (1997); Montgomery, Ten Lectures (1994); Iwaniec and Kowalski, Analytic Number Theory
// (2004); Graham and Kolesnik, Van der Corput's Method of Exponential Sums (1991); Tenenbaum, Introduction to Analytic
// and Probabilistic Number Theory (3rd ed. 2015); Khinchin, Continued Fractions (1964); Einsiedler and Ward, Ergodic
// Theory with a view towards Number Theory (2011); Crandall and Pomerance, Prime Numbers (2nd ed. 2005); Cohen, A Course
// in Computational Algebraic Number Theory (1993); Borwein and Erdélyi, Polynomials and Polynomial Inequalities (1995);
// Smyth, The Mahler measure of algebraic numbers: a survey (2008). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

